# Untitled

Centimetre-scale micropore alignment in oriented polycrystalline metal-organic framework films via heteroepitaxial growth
The fabrication of oriented, crystalline films of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) is a critical step toward their application to advanced technologies such as optics, microelectronics, microfluidics and sensing. However, the direct synthesis of MOF films with controlled crystalline orientation remains a significant challenge. Here we report a one-step approach, carried out under mild conditions, that exploits heteroepitaxial growth for the rapid fabrication of oriented polycrystalline MOF films on the centimetre scale. Our methodology employs crystalline copper hydroxide as a substrate and yields MOF films with oriented pore channels on scales that primarily depend on the dimensions of the substrate. To demonstrate that an anisotropic crystalline morphology can translate to a functional property, we assembled a centimetre-scale MOF film in the presence of a dye and showed that the optical response could be switched 'ON' or 'OFF' by simply rotating the film.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also called porous coordination polymers, are a class of crystalline materials that commonly exhibit remarkably high accessible surface areas and pore volumes<sup>1,2</sup>. A key aspect of MOF chemistry is that their modular construction, from organic links and metal nodes, allows for control of pore chemistry<sup>3,4</sup>, structure metrics<sup>5</sup> and network topology<sup>6</sup>. Accordingly, MOF architectures can be specifically designed for application to areas such as gas storage<sup>5</sup>, catalysis<sup>7</sup>, drug delivery<sup>8</sup>, sensing<sup>9,10</sup>, optics and light harvesting<sup>11–13</sup> and molecular separations<sup>14</sup>. An important step towards fully realizing the potential of MOFs in advanced technologies is the development of fabrication methods that allow for structural alignment to be transferred from individual crystals to continuous films of centimetre dimensions<sup>15–17</sup>. In general, processing porous materials into films on such scales has been acknowledged as essential for their integration into functional devices<sup>18–21</sup>. Here we show that a heteroepitaxial growth mechanism can be used to prepare, centimetre-scale, aligned two-dimensional (2D) and 3D crystalline MOF films directly from an inorganic substrate, \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). Remarkably, this approach gives rise to unprecedented structural orientation of a continuous MOF film in both the in-plane (a and b axes) and out-of-plane (c axis) directions affording an oriented polycrystalline morphology. Such precise alignment, along all the axes, is highly desired for optical<sup>13</sup>, sensor<sup>22,23</sup> and microelectronic<sup>24,25</sup> applications where control of both position and orientation of functional building blocks within the framework architecture is necessary. To exemplify the unique anisotropic properties of the heteroepitaxially grown MOFs described in this work we align a fluorescent dye to a specific crystallographic plane of
a continuous MOF film and show that the intensity of the fluorescent response is dependent on the angle between the linearly polarized excitation radiation and the crystal lattice planes. Thus, simply by rotating the MOF film the fluorescent signal can be switched 'ON' or 'OFF'.
## Background on MOF film fabrication methods
A number of fabrication methods have been developed for the deposition of MOF films and patterns; however, the growth of MOF crystals that are oriented along all crystallographic axes has not been explicitly demonstrated<sup>26–34</sup>. For example, liquid phase epitaxy on organically functionalized surfaces has been used to grow MOF crystals with out-of-plane orientation<sup>28–32,35</sup> only; in-plane crystallographic orientation of individual MOF crystallites was not confirmed. On the other hand, a few examples of MOF crystals with in-plane orientations have been reported but these are limited to average crystalline domains of tens of nanometres<sup>36,37</sup>. It is also noteworthy that the aforementioned approaches employed organic molecules as a buffer layer to promote framework growth<sup>28,30,37</sup>. Lastly, another constraint of these techniques is that MOF crystal growth is arduously controlled via stepwise 'layer-by-layer' addition of the building units (that is, inorganic nodes and organic ligands). Here we synthesize a MOF film oriented along the a, b and c crystallographic axes, in minutes, via conversion of a crystalline metal hydroxide surface. The resulting macroscopic film possesses a precisely aligned micropore structure, thus allowing the established chemistry of MOF crystals to be translated to the macroscale. Furthermore, an important discovery garnered from this work is that alignment of the nascent MOF film occurs via a heteroepitaxial
growth process that is facilitated by precise matching of the structure metrics of the organic linkers and the lattice parameters of the metal hydroxide substrate. We note that previous reports of epitaxial MOF growth that rely on using a preformed MOF as the substrate for generating the new lattice30,39 are distinct from this present work as using the inorganic substrate offers rapid, single-step syntheses of crystalline MOF films on a commercially viable scale.
